text:
marta: Quick heads-up, customer 'Acadia' hitting a heavy burst of 429s during CI prefetch on deploy. They enable prefetch in our browser SDK and run ~48 parallel runners.

alex: Is this immediately on deploy or a later warmup job?

marta: At deploy start. They prefetch tokens in a separate step to avoid cold starts. CI log excerpt:```
[INFO] prefetch: requesting ephemeral token
[WARN] 429 Too Many Requests - /v1/ephemeral_tokens (x8)
[DEBUG] retry in 120ms
[DEBUG] retry in 160ms
[ERROR] retries exhausted after 5 attempts
```

devon: Classic thundering herd. If all runners call at once you get a stampede. Do they use SDK default backoff?

marta: Yes, vanilla config. They flipped prefetch true to shave startup time.

alex: Short-term options: 1) ask infra for a temporary burst increase for their tenant during the rollout; 2) advise client to add a single-flight token fetch + jitter; 3) flip prefetch off and do staged rollout. Which should we pursue now?

marta: They want minimal disruption, so request infra burst first and send a quick client patch.

ratebot: [alert] tenant acadia-corp ephemeral_token rate spike detected: 10x baseline in 3m. Auto-throttle engaged.

alex: I'll open BR ticket and tag infra-oncall. Suggest 5x allowed burst for 15 minutes starting at their rollout. If infra can't, ask for a short progressive ramp.

alex: Ticket BR-9471 opened and assigned to infra-oncall.

infra-oncall: Acknowledge. Reviewing now. If we can't raise global cap we can carve a temporary exception for their tenant for 10 minutes.

devon: Meanwhile paste this tiny client patch to them — it avoids parallel token storms by ensuring only one fetch per process and adds randomized backoff:```js
let pending = null;
async function fetchEphemeralOnce() {
  if (pending) return pending;
  pending = (async () => {
    for (let i=0;i<6;i++) {
      try { return await requestToken(); } catch (e) {
        const backoff = Math.floor(Math.random()*200) + (Math.pow(2,i)*100);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, backoff));
      }
    }
    throw new Error('ephemeral token fetch failed');
  })();
  try { return await pending; } finally { pending = null; }
}
```

marta: Sending that + instructions to stagger runner start by hashing build id across runners so starts are spread over ~30s.

alex: Good. Also propose they change prefetch to "staggered" mode in our SDK (we should add that option to docs).

infra-oncall: BR-9471 update: we can grant a tenant-scoped burst for 12 minutes, effective immediately. Monitor ratebot feed and let us know if errors persist.

ratebot: [update] BR-9471 applied: tenant acadia-corp ephemeral_token limit bumped x4 for 12m.

marta: I'll coordinate with Acadia's SREs and ask them to redeploy. I'll report back within 40m.

devon: One ops takeaway: consider adding a Retry-After header with a small randomized jitter from the service to help clients avoid synchronized retries. I'll spin up a short RFC for the ephemeral endpoint behavior.

alex: Also capture this as a CS playbook entry: "prefetch stampede during onboarding" — include mitigation steps (client single-flight snippet, staggered starts, when to request infra burst, rollback to staged deploy). Marta, can you draft the playbook?

marta: On it. I'll add the CI config they shared and a short post-mortem after the rollout. Will also push a doc note for "staggered prefetch" in our SDK guide. :thumbsup:
